use reviews
use rules.engine.decide
use rules.policy.Decision
use rules.policy.PullRequest

/// Decides one pull request the host built, and answers what it decided.
///
/// This is the function the Rust host in `examples/rules/host/` invokes, once
/// per application request, against a module it compiled and lowered once. It
/// is the signature issue #90 wrote down and issue #150 could not call:
/// `Vm::invoke` and `Interpreter::invoke` take a `Vec<Value>`, so the host
/// builds a `rules.policy.PullRequest` and hands it over, and reads the
/// `rules.policy.Decision` back out of the answer.
///
/// Nothing crosses the Host API boundary here, and that is the point rather
/// than an omission. The pull request arrives as an argument and the decision
/// leaves as a result, so this entry needs no host module, asks for no
/// capability, and makes no call a trace would record. What an embedding
/// wanted a boundary for was carrying values in and out, and a boundary is a
/// poor way to carry values.
///
/// `decideRequest` below is the same decision reached the other way, and it
/// stays because the difference between the two is what
/// `examples/rules/README.md` measures.
export fn evaluate(pr: PullRequest) -> Decision {
  decide(pr)
}

/// Decides the one pull request the host names, and reports the decision back
/// to it.
///
/// The control. This reaches `decide` over a pull request fetched through
/// `reviews.pull` and reports the answer through `reviews.record`, so
/// everything it does that `evaluate` does not is the Host API boundary. The
/// embedding measures the two against each other, which is what turns "the
/// boundary costs something" into a number.
///
/// It was once the only way in. `run_entry` takes `Vec<Rc<str>>` on both
/// backends, so the request identifier had to arrive as a process argument
/// and the pull request had to come back out through a host call; that was
/// issue #150, and `evaluate` above is what closed it. What the shape is
/// still good for is a second thing this entry demonstrates: both `reviews`
/// operations take the identifier as their first argument, so every
/// `HostCall` event a trace sink sees carries it and a run's calls group by
/// application request without the runtime knowing what an application
/// request is.
///
/// The decision leaves by both routes at once, on purpose. It is returned, so
/// the host reads a typed `rules.policy.Decision` out of the run's answer, and
/// it is also handed to `reviews.record`, so the same decision crosses the
/// boundary in the other direction under the request identifier that started
/// it.
export fn decideRequest(args: Array<String>) -> Result<Decision, Error> {
  let requestId = args.get(0).unwrapOr("")
  let decision = decide(pullRequest(requestId)?)
  reviews.record(
    requestId,
    decision.policy.name(),
    decision.policy.reviewers(),
    decision.trail(),
  )?
  Ok(decision)
}

/// Fetches the pull request the host holds under `requestId`, and answers
/// nothing else.
///
/// The embedding invokes this on its own as the control that separates what
/// the boundary costs from what the rules cost: it makes the same inbound
/// call and the same conversion as `decideRequest` and then weighs nothing.
export fn pullOnly(args: Array<String>) -> Result<PullRequest, Error> {
  pullRequest(args.get(0).unwrapOr(""))
}

/// One pull request, converted from what the host declared into what this
/// package declared.
///
/// The two types are not the same type and could not be. `reviews.PullRequest`
/// is a `cove_schema::TypeSchema` the embedder wrote in Rust, and
/// `rules.policy.PullRequest` is a struct this package declared; a Host API
/// schema has no way to name a type a Cove package owns. So the ten fields
/// are written out once, here, where a field the host stops sending is a
/// check-time error at the line that reads it.
///
/// Every field crosses as the shape it is. `labels` is a `Set<String>` on
/// both sides and is copied across like any other field: it used to cross as
/// an `Array` and be walked into a `Set` wherever a rule asked a membership
/// question, because `HostType` had no `Set` to declare one with, and issue
/// #153 closed that.
fn pullRequest(requestId: String) -> Result<PullRequest, Error> {
  let supplied = reviews.pull(requestId)?
  Ok(PullRequest(id: supplied.id, title: supplied.title, author: supplied.author, targetBranch: supplied.targetBranch, changedLines: supplied.changedLines, filesTouched: supplied.filesTouched, labels: supplied.labels, approvals: supplied.approvals, isDraft: supplied.isDraft, hasTests: supplied.hasTests))
}
